text=f'''# fsQCA pre-analysis report — protocol proposed, not approved

Article: **Financing the Renewable Energy Transition: Financial Development, Institutional Quality, and Income-Group Heterogeneity**.

The panel findings remain unchanged. This is a separate, temporally ordered country-case design, not an attempt to rescue inconclusive financial-depth, complementarity or income-interaction tests. No fuzzy country membership, empirical truth table, necessity/sufficiency test or minimization has been calculated. Numeric anchors and the primary-outcome choice were frozen at **{P['frozen_utc']}**, before the new raw threshold diagnostics; the original protocol hash is retained.

## Cases, windows and missingness

Use arithmetic means of financial depth, RQ and real GDP per capita over **2018–2020**, followed by financing outcomes over **2021–2023**. Each country/economy appears once. Require all three actual observations for each condition and financing, nonnegative financing, positive real GDP per capita and positive annual population, plus a known 2018 income classification. Do not require 2018–2020 financing: that would introduce an unnecessary extra selection criterion. Mean per-capita financing is the mean of the three annual financing/population ratios, not a ratio of separately averaged series. A separately labelled ratio-of-sums alternative is retained for denominator sensitivity.

{md(cov,['income_group_2018','economies','eligible_cases','excluded','baseline_FD_missing','baseline_RQ_missing','baseline_GDPpc_missing_or_nonpositive','financing_window_incomplete','stable_income_cases'])}

There are **111 eligible cases**: 24 low-income, 41 lower-middle-income, 35 upper-middle-income and 11 high-income, classified at baseline 2018. The delivered `fsqca_case_dataset.csv` contains only those eligible cases, with original annual values, flags and both outcome concepts; the all-217-economy exclusion ledger remains in audit evidence and the workbook. Missingness categories overlap. Ninety-five economies lack a complete financing window; no unavailable financing year is recoded to zero. Published 0.00 entries remain numerical observations with rounding caution, not established national zero finance.

Only **11 of 80** baseline high-income economies are eligible; 68 have an incomplete financing window. Nine eligible high-income cases retain their income classification through 2023. Across all cases 87 retain their 2018 classification through 2023. This source selection is substantial and cannot be remedied by fuzzy calibration. Retain historical group changes and entity/sovereign flags. Do not infer high-income national investment patterns or count repeated country-years as extra cases. Do not conduct four separate group minimizations with eleven high-income cases.

## Primary outcome: intensity, not size or national transition success

Recommend **high recorded selected-public renewable-finance intensity per resident**, measured as mean annual constant-2022 USD commitments per person over 2021–2023. The theoretical question concerns how enabling institutions, bank intermediation and domestic resource capacity combine with materially available finance relative to population. With no country-size condition in the initial three-condition design, absolute funding volume would mechanically favor larger national portfolios. Per-capita scaling addresses that construct issue; it does not assert equal allocation or benefit to residents, measure fiscal effort, or turn selected public commitments into total investment. Small-population project lumpiness remains a limitation.

Keep **large absolute financing volume** as a distinct alternative: mean annual commitments in million constant-2022 USD (and separately labelled three-year cumulative total). It addresses the mobilization of a substantial national project-finance portfolio. Do not combine absolute/per-capita outcomes in one set, pool resulting solutions, call either installed capacity, or select one after comparing solution significance/consistency. A large absolute amount can coexist with a modest per-capita amount, and vice versa. The primary choice was made on this construct distinction before diagnostics.

## Proposed calibration anchors

{md(anchors,['set_name','unit','full_out','crossover','full_in'])}

These are **researcher-proposed conceptual/materiality thresholds**, not official institutional classifications or externally validated universal cutoffs. Source definitions cannot manufacture an objective dividing line between low and high country-level financing. Approval should explicitly assess these numerical judgments and, where available, bring substantive country/project-finance knowledge to the materiality boundaries. Do not present this draft as publication-cleared merely because thresholds are round or produce cases on both sides.

- **Banking depth 20/50/100% GDP:** credit is a stock relative to annual output, not new credit issued each year. One-fifth, one-half and approximately one year of GDP distinguish limited, intermediate and extensive bank intermediation on an economic scale. This calibrates depth, not access, efficiency, optimal credit or a universally beneficial financial system. The WB source supports the construct, not these numerical boundaries.
- **RQ −1/0/+1:** define relatively weak/reference/strong governance-estimate locations. Under revised WGI methodology the global mean may vary; zero must not be described as the exact annual world average. These are relative-location proposals, not a universal policy adequacy standard. The frozen governance-estimate scale is used throughout; no percentile or 0–100 score is substituted. Baseline mean-estimate standard errors are not available, and annual uncertainty must not be divided by sqrt(3) under an unsupported independence assumption.
- **Real GDP per capita 2,000/8,000/20,000 constant-2015 USD:** calibrate limited, intermediate and substantial domestic aggregate resource capacity, not household welfare or PPP purchasing power. These resource-scale judgments need substantive approval. Historical WB groups are based on Atlas GNI in current USD; their thresholds must not be copied into this real-GDP measure. Contextual group labels and the wealth/resource condition are distinct.
- **Per-capita outcome 1/10/50 constant-2022 USD per resident per year:** propose minor, intermediate and substantial recorded commitment envelopes relative to population. No institution certifies these as adequate renewable-transition funding. They are deliberately labelled materiality proposals, not estimated needs, project costs or per-capita welfare gains.
- **Absolute outcome 1/25/100 million constant-2022 USD per year:** propose a small national ticket, an intermediate portfolio and sustained large funding volume. Three-year cumulative counterparts are 3/75/300 million, not the same numeric thresholds. Public project-finance ticket sizes and country needs vary, so the qualitative meaning must be reviewed before approval.

GDP amounts stay in constant-2015 USD, financing in constant-2022 USD. They are separate constructs; their dollar anchors are not numerically interchangeable. No conversion to capacity/generation or financing needs is made to justify thresholds.

## Calibration function, alternatives and crossover sensitivity

After approval, the proposed direct method maps exclusion/crossover/inclusion anchors to **0.05/0.50/0.95**, avoiding literal 0/1 certainty. For raw value x and anchors l<c<u, use logistic(log(19)×(x−c)/(c−l)) below c and logistic(log(19)×(x−c)/(u−c)) at/above c. These equations explicitly fix the interpolation rather than relying on unspecified package defaults. Missing x stays missing. All countries use the same set definitions and anchors; group-specific quantiles would change the meaning of high across groups and are not proposed.

Prespecified one-at-a-time crossover alternatives: depth **40/60**, RQ **−0.25/+0.25**, GDPpc **6,000/10,000**, per-capita finance **5/20**, and absolute finance **10/50**, with the primary endpoints held fixed. A secondary shape sensitivity can use log distance for strictly positive depth/GDPpc and log1p(x/one stated unit) for nonnegative financing, always preserving the raw-unit anchors and declaring the reference unit. Do not construct a Cartesian threshold search or choose a curve by attractive solutions. Review any request to revise the proposed numbers before configuration inspection, retain the old protocol and justify the revision independently of solutions.

The workbook reports raw values near primary/alternative crossovers in fixed bands: depth ±5 percentage points, RQ ±0.1, GDPpc ±800, intensity ±1 USD/person/year and absolute volume ±2.5 million. These bands are descriptive case-review buffers, not WGI confidence intervals. It also shows raw one-variable counts below/above cutoffs. **No multivariate configuration occupancy or fuzzy memberships were computed.** At a future exact crossover, membership remains 0.5; never add 0.001 mechanically to force a preferred side. Near-crossover cases require substantive interpretation and sensitivity disclosure.

## Anticipated diversity, theory and analysis gate

Three conditions imply eight possible binary combinations, but 111 cases do not guarantee that all combinations are supported. Depth, governance and income/resource capacity can covary, creating limited diversity and contradictory cases. Raw correlations and variable ranges are retained as preparation diagnostics, not criteria for deleting inconvenient conditions. The scarce high-income cases further limit contextual comparisons. No observed configuration counts are claimed before approval.

Rival theoretical expectations should remain visible. Strong RQ can reassure public providers and improve project implementation; deeper banking can support cofinancing. Yet selected public finance can also target weak banking systems, low domestic resource capacity or financing gaps. High GDPpc may permit national financing while reducing eligibility for external development finance. Thus temporal ordering does not justify forcing every condition to have a positive direction or using unsupported counterfactual remainders. These are theoretical hypotheses for case interpretation, not configurations extracted from the data.

For review, propose a pooled primary truth-table frequency threshold of **3** (2 and 5 sensitivity), sufficiency consistency **0.80**, PRI **0.65**, and necessity screening consistency **0.90** with coverage/relevance checks. These are **future methodological proposals**, not calculated statistics or automatic proof. Approve them with the calibration protocol and report low coverage, contradictions and limited diversity rather than optimizing them away. Keep negative-outcome analysis, if approved, separate; absence of high observed commitments is not evidence of no national financing. No unsupported logical remainders should be treated as facts.

## Complementary role and preservation

fsQCA can examine whether combinations of measured baseline resources and institutions accompany later high observed public-finance intensity, allowing asymmetric patterns and detailed case review. It does not estimate the panel's marginal slopes, test the same null hypotheses, validate inconclusive interactions, or establish causal necessity/sufficiency. A configurational association consistent with a theory would remain subject to recipient selection, measurement uncertainty, three-year commitment timing, provider coverage and unobserved confounding. Mixed methods can illuminate different questions while preserving uncertainty in both.

The frozen panel/results remain untouched. Required outputs are in this new preparation folder with reproducible R/Python scripts and evidence. **Review and explicitly approve the calibration protocol before any case calibration, empirical truth table, necessity/sufficiency testing or minimization.** No solutions are reported here.
'''
(OUT/'fsqca_pre_analysis_report.md').write_text(text,encoding='utf-8');print('Protocol workbook payload',len(payload['fsqca_calibration_protocol.xlsx']),'sheets; report written')
